"paragraphs": [
  "VWAP as the name says is a moving average (MA) that is based on volume being traded. The more the volume gets traded the more aggressively this MA is going to follow the price, and if the volume is low this MA will follow the price slower. It\u2019s the true average price of an asset during the trading day because it counts both the price and how many contracts being traded at that price. Think of it as a \"fair value\" benchmark line on the chart: if millions of contracts trade at a certain price, the line gets pulled heavily toward that number. Big institutions use it as a guide, considering any price below the VWAP line a good discount and any price above it an expensive premium.",

    "The VWAP indicator instantiates the mean reversion effect due to a lack of big-money backing causes the price to lose momentum while it stretches away from the mean like a rubber band, thus triggering a mean-reversion effect whereby it gets sucked right back into the mean, i.e. the VWAP line.",
    "As a retail trader one can readily leverage the VWAP triggered S/R trade \u2014trading the price bounces off the VWAP line alongside institutions\u2014or by trading the rubber band snap especially by measuring how many  (standard deviations) away the price has deviated from the \u2018mean\u2019 when it gets unsustainably overextended into the outer bands to ride it back to the mean which is the VWAP level."
  ]
},

