"Atlas Momentum": {
  "title": "Atlas Momentum",
  "subsections": {},
  "paragraphs": [

    "In trading, momentum is the pure velocity and speedometer of the market, measuring how fast & aggressively price is moving in a specific direction regardless of its cyclic rhythm. If oscillation is a playground swing moving back & forth, momentum is a freight train barreling down the tracks; it tells you how much raw horsepower and crowd energy is driving a move, signaling that a trend is so powerful it will likely blast right through normal turning points. For a new trader, tracking momentum helps you jump on board fast-moving market waves early and stay in the trade for maximum profit, as long as that speedometer stays dialed into high gear.",
    "Table Data: [[\"Salient Features\\nMomentum Histograms: The Atlas Momentum indicator is plotted vertical histograms as green for bull / red for bear runs.\\nRising Histograms \\u2013 Bull or Bear: The vertically rising histograms color determines either the bear or a bull run. \\nFor strong momentum in the bear run (prices moving down) the RED histograms would be rising.\\nAnd same is the case for bull run (rising prices) the GREEN histograms would also be rising.\\nTherefore, it\\u2019s the color of the histogram that differentiates between the bull or a bear run, both histograms are rising.\\nMomentum Follow-through: The Atlas Momentum histogram is generally a follow-through of the Atlas Oscillator. So, first Atlas Oscillator starts the move before the price movement is evident. Then the Atlas Momentum histograms follows-through.\\nStrength/Weakness in Momentum: The height and shape of the histogram\\u2019s tops / peaks are very crucial that determine the strength or weakness of the momentum in a move (bull/bear):\\nRising histograms peaks are for stronger momentum\\nDeclining histogram peaks are weak momentum\\nFalling histogram peaks may also mean trend reversal\\nSaturated Momentum Trend: Saturated histograms peaks correspond to saturated momentum and thus deep into the trend strength that stipulates for a high probability trade. To recognize a saturated momentum, we see the histogram peaks:\\nThe shape of a saturated histogram peaks form like rising dome.\\nFor a longer saturation dome sometime after certain candle, it appears to be slightly declining but still saturate, show the trend is still deeply powerful.\"]]"
  ]
},

    "Table Data: [[\"Salient Features\\nMomentum Histograms: The Atlas Momentum indicator is plotted vertical histograms as green for bull / red for bear runs.\\nRising Histograms \\u2013 Bull or Bear: The vertically rising histograms color determines either the bear or a bull run. \\nFor strong momentum in the bear run (prices moving down) the RED histograms would be rising.\\nAnd same is the case for bull run (rising prices) the GREEN histograms would also be rising.\\nTherefore, it\\u2019s the color of the histogram that differentiates between the bull or a bear run, both histograms are rising.\\nMomentum Follow-through: The Atlas Momentum histogram is generally a follow-through of the Atlas Oscillator. So, first Atlas Oscillator starts the move before the price movement is evident. Then the Atlas Momentum histograms follows-through.\\nStrength/Weakness in Momentum: The height and shape of the histogram\\u2019s tops / peaks are very crucial that determine the strength or weakness of the momentum in a move (bull/bear):\\nRising histograms peaks are for stronger momentum\\nDeclining histogram peaks are weak momentum\\nFalling histogram peaks may also mean trend reversal\\nSaturated Momentum Trend: Saturated histograms peaks correspond to saturated momentum and thus deep into the trend strength that stipulates for a high probability trade. To recognize a saturated momentum, we see the histogram peaks:\\nThe shape of a saturated histogram peaks form like rising dome.\\nFor a longer saturation dome sometime after certain candle, it appears to be slightly declining but still saturate, show the trend is still deeply powerful.\"]]"
