"Atlas Explosion": {
  "title": "Atlas Explosion",
  "subsections": {},
  "paragraphs": [

    "Explosion phenomenon proves that institutional buying or selling volume has officially broken out of its recent volatility cage. The indicator acts as a dynamic volatility hurdle that automatically adjusts to market conditions, rising during chaotic price swings. It\u2019s a further breakout within a trend direction and momentum piercing above it, signaling that the breakout has enough mathematical force to sustain a massive trend run. This is once the oscillation has confirmed the move, and the momentum showing strong, there occurs a chaotic breakout in that direction make this trade a high probability trade. To catch an explosive market breakout, you ignore the price chart and focus strictly on this moving line inside your indicator window, waiting for this explosion to occur.",
    "Table Data: [[\"Salient Features\\nExplosion Plot \\u2013 Cyan/Gray: Within the same sub-plot at the top the thin Cyan-Gray line rising and falling is the Atlas Explosion line. The rising explosion line would be colored cyan, falling would gray.\\nExplosion Saturation \\u2013 ~150: The value range of the explosion plot is from 0-150. After reaching top ~150 it reaches the saturation point and plot clips down at around 100 (saturation settlement value) and stays there in cyan until the explosion is over. Once the explosion is over it falls back down as gray. One could stay in trade until this time when it falls back to gray from this saturation point.\\nExplosion Detection \\u2013 Long or Short: It is important to understand the explosion does NOT show the direction bias of the trade whether to be long or short. It only signals further explosion in the direction of the oscillation & momentum; therefore, oscillation line & momentum histograms need to be favorable first before jumping upon an explosion line. \\nExplosion Follow-through: As indicated earlier section Oscillation being the first to signal a direction that is then followed through by momentum. By that time if the trend strength is still deep enough it could potentially produce an explosion. So usually, explosion is the follow-through of momentum. Sometimes it\\u2019s possible to have explosion with a lack of momentum, in such cases it would be better to ignore such an explosion as that could be short lived. The full-saturation explosion, however, demands for a full-blown dome-shaped strong momentum, and deep oscillation.\"]]"
  ]
},

