  "title": "Altas Recent Highs & Lows \u2013 Microstructure S/R [Dashed Horizontal Lines]",
  "subsections": {},
  "paragraphs": [

    "The Atlas Recent High-Low component automatically locks solid horizontal reference lines onto your chart to map the exact structural peaks and troughs where institutional order flow shifts. Algorithms constantly monitor these staircase boundaries because they contain dense clusters of retail resting liquidity\u2014specifically, buy-stops stacked above the red resistance levels and sell-stops resting below the green support levels. Instead of treating these coordinates as random barriers, you use them as dynamic trading triggers: you enter a momentum breakout when price cleanly penetrates a level on heavy volume, or you execute a high-reward reversion fade when an algorithm sweeps the high/low only to face immediate absorption and a sharp wick rejection back into the range.",
    "Figure : Atlas Breakout Indicator - Recent Highs & Lows"
  ]
}

      "Figure : Atlas Breakout Indicator - Recent Highs & Lows"
    ]
  }
},
"paragraphs": [

  }
},
"paragraphs": [
  "The breakout strategy across oblique trendlines and recent highs or lows relies on entering positions as price breaches these structural thresholds, anticipating rapid directional expansion. However, because retail day traders universally place their protective stop-losses and breakout market orders directly around these highly visible boundaries, they create dense, predictable pools of liquidity. While horizontal highs and lows form concentrated stacks of orders at a single price coordinate, oblique trendlines distribute a staggered gradient of liquidity along their slope. Institutional execution algorithms actively exploit this structural layout by intentionally driving price past these lines to trigger a domino effect of retail stops and breakout entries. The algorithm cleanly absorbs these cascading market orders to fill its own massive, passive block positions; once this localized retail liquidity is completely dissipated, the artificial buying or selling pressure vanishes, causing the market to violently reverse and leave the breakout traders trapped at the absolute extreme.",

    "Figure : Atlas Breakout Indicator - Atlas Trend Line S/R, Atlas Previous High's & Low's",
    "On the Master Chart as well as Entry Charts on the main price panel along where the candles are plotted, the Atlas Breakout Indicator automatically plots these Market Structural Breakout lines, that is the oblique trend lines and Recent High\u2019s and Lows:"
  ]
},
