{
  "type": "paragraph",

},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "VWAP as the name says is a moving average (MA) that is based on volume being traded. The more the volume gets traded the more aggressively this MA is going to follow the price, and if the volume is low this MA will follow the price slower. It\u2019s the true average price of an asset during the trading day because it counts both the price and how many contracts being traded at that price. Think of it as a \"fair value\" benchmark line on the chart: if millions of contracts trade at a certain price, the line gets pulled heavily toward that number. Big institutions use it as a guide, considering any price below the VWAP line a good discount and any price above it an expensive premium.",

  "h1": "Atlas VWAP \u2013 Volume-Weighted Average Price",
  "h2": "",
  "h3": ""
},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "In general, it is a powerful magnet as well as S/R line. As it represents the day's fair wholesale price where massive institutional algorithms are programmed to buy & sell. When the market dips down to the VWAP line, institutions flood in to buy a good deal, creating a floor of support; when the price stretches too far away from this line, institutional trading completely stops because the price is no longer a bargain, therefore it becomes a support. Same is the case for it to be a resistance line.",
  "h1": "Atlas VWAP \u2013 Volume-Weighted Average Price",
  "h2": "",
  "h3": ""
},
{
  "type": "paragraph",

},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "The VWAP indicator instantiates the mean reversion effect due to a lack of big-money backing causes the price to lose momentum while it stretches away from the mean like a rubber band, thus triggering a mean-reversion effect whereby it gets sucked right back into the mean, i.e. the VWAP line.",
  "h1": "Atlas VWAP \u2013 Volume-Weighted Average Price",
  "h2": "",
  "h3": ""
},
{
  "type": "paragraph",

},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "As a retail trader one can readily leverage the VWAP triggered S/R trade \u2014trading the price bounces off the VWAP line alongside institutions\u2014or by trading the rubber band snap especially by measuring how many  (standard deviations) away the price has deviated from the \u2018mean\u2019 when it gets unsustainably overextended into the outer bands to ride it back to the mean which is the VWAP level.",
  "h1": "Atlas VWAP \u2013 Volume-Weighted Average Price",
  "h2": "",
  "h3": "The Trade Opportunity:"
},
{
  "type": "paragraph",

},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "The Atlas VWAP usually plotted as a thick dash-dot-dot pattern in a green, red or a goldenrod color along with a following band with some deterministic thickness attached to it. The indicator also tracked with a slope m and a standard deviate  value in the Atlas Regime Dashboard",
  "h1": "Atlas VWAP \u2013 Volume-Weighted Average Price",
  "h2": "Atlas Propriety Features \u2013 Atlas VWAP",
  "h3": ""
},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "Atlas VWAP has a few unique complementary features:",
