  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "At its core, the\u00a0Atlas Bulls & Bears Indicator\u00a0is designed to show you where the real heavy hitters\u2014the institutions, central banks, and market makers\u2014are moving their capital. Retail traders simply don't have the financial muscle to move markets long-term; significant, sustained price trends only happen when large institutions inject massive liquidity pressure into the order book. This indicator captures that massive footprint in real-time, tracking how fast these large-scale market orders are aggressively sweeping through available supply and demand. By showing you exactly when institutional money flow is overwhelming the market, it fits perfectly into your overall strategy as a master trend. It takes the guesswork out of your trading by telling you precisely when to ride the coattails of institutional momentum rallies, when to stay flat during quiet neutral ranges, and when a trend has become so overextended that it's smart to take profits before a major reversal hits.",

},
{
  "type": "paragraph",
  "text": "The breakout strategy across oblique trendlines and recent highs or lows relies on entering positions as price breaches these structural thresholds, anticipating rapid directional expansion. However, because retail day traders universally place their protective stop-losses and breakout market orders directly around these highly visible boundaries, they create dense, predictable pools of liquidity. While horizontal highs and lows form concentrated stacks of orders at a single price coordinate, oblique trendlines distribute a staggered gradient of liquidity along their slope. Institutional execution algorithms actively exploit this structural layout by intentionally driving price past these lines to trigger a domino effect of retail stops and breakout entries. The algorithm cleanly absorbs these cascading market orders to fill its own massive, passive block positions; once this localized retail liquidity is completely dissipated, the artificial buying or selling pressure vanishes, causing the market to violently reverse and leave the breakout traders trapped at the absolute extreme.",

